[11:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going where we go from here, the future\'s going to be very interesting. I personally think there\'s still a huge use case for musicians to have a direct-to-fan package that\'s scarce, that\'s limited'

[11:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'editions, that offers fans something exclusive that you they can\'t get anywhere else. But, I think the NFT boom was also for music because of the secondary market. Every time you'

[11:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'resold an NFT, the original musician would get a royalty rate. Whereas, if you buy a record and you sell it on eBay, that original artist doesn\'t get a royalty.'

[11:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Whereas, for NFTs, it did. There was also proof of ownership in terms of it being on the blockchain. So, you could actually see the transactions on the blockchain. And the music wasn\'t held on there. The images wasn\'t held on there. They\'re all'

[12:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'held on decentralized servers that connected it to that blockchain entry or transaction ID. Um So, yeah. In terms of what artists should do'

